# Clean Code

Source: Robert C. Martin, _Clean Code_. Inventory: `docs/clean-code-inventory.md`.

## Meaningful Names (Ch.2)

Prefer intention-revealing, searchable, pronounceable names. One word per concept. No Hungarian / type prefixes. Classes and types are nouns; functions are verbs (React components stay PascalCase nouns). File names are kebab-case (`match-card.tsx`, `use-now.ts`); Expo Router reserved names (`_layout`, `+html`, `(tabs)`) stay as the framework requires. Prefer problem-domain vocabulary in `data/`, `modules/` interactors/presenters; solution-domain (Expo / React Native) words in `app/` routes, hooks, and Views.

### Domain vocabulary

- `Game`, `GameId`, `Team`, `Match` — Entity DTOs in `data/types` / `data/games`
- `MatchStatus` (`live` / `soon` / `upcoming` / `finished`), `DayGroup`, `GameFilter`
- Kickoff, countdown, tournament, format (`Bo3` / `Bo5`)
- VIPER modules: Schedule (`modules/schedule/`), Games (`modules/games/`)
- Builders: `app/(tabs)/index.tsx` (Schedule), `app/(tabs)/games.tsx` (Games)
- Clock infrastructure: `lib/use-now.ts`

## Functions (Ch.3)

Small; do one thing; one abstraction level; stepdown (caller above callee). Prefer 0–2 args; at most 3 positional — more → a named argument object type. Avoid flag arguments. No hidden side effects. Command/query separation. Prefer extract within the VIPER role (Interactor / Presenter helpers) over growing past size gates. Keep Views thin: Interactor owns use cases, Presenter owns view models, View renders props.

## Comments (Ch.4)

Prefer rename/extract over narrative comments. Keep legal, intent, clarification, and warning comments. Ban `FIXME` / `XXX` / `HACK`; temporary `TODO` OK with why. Delete commented-out code.

## Formatting (Ch.5)

Prettier is the horizontal source of truth (`npx expo lint` + `.prettierrc`). Vertical: newspaper metaphor, single blank between concepts, density within a thought, dependents below callers, conceptual affinity. Blank line around `if`/`for`, before `return`, and after a declaration group before the next kind of statement (`padding-line-between-statements`). Do not fight Prettier with other style ESLint.

## Objects and Data Structures (Ch.6)

Objects hide data behind operations; data structures expose data. `Match`, `Team`, and `Game` are DTOs — no behavior on the payload. Status, countdown, and day grouping live in the Schedule Interactor / Presenter. Law of Demeter: talk to neighbors, not strangers — reading DTO fields (`match.teamA.name`) is fine.

## Error Handling (Ch.7)

Prefer exceptions over error codes for unexpected failures (`getGame` throwing on unknown `GameId` is the pattern). No empty catches. Provide context. Don’t use throw for expected validation — return a named outcome (`GameFilter | null`). Be deliberate with null/optional.

## Boundaries (Ch.8)

Wrap third-party APIs behind owned façades. Sample `MATCHES` is a stand-in: a live schedule API belongs behind `data/` + Schedule Interactor, not inside Views. Learning tests at the boundary. Expo Router (`Link`, `router`, `useLocalSearchParams`) stays in `app/` as Router adapters / Builders. Features depend on what you control (module contracts).

## Classes (Ch.10)

Small, cohesive, single responsibility. This app is function- and type-first VIPER (contracts + functions); don’t introduce classes unless they earn cohesion. Organize for change. Expo Router layout / `ErrorBoundary` conventions stay framework-owned when they conflict with purity.

## Systems (Ch.11)

Separate construction from use. Builders in `app/` wire View, Presenter, Interactor, and Router adapters. One-way VIPER dependencies:

- `app/` → `modules/`, `lib/use-now`, `constants/`, `expo-router` (and Entity types when adapting)
- Presenter → Interactor, Entity types, Router contract
- Interactor → `data/` (Games Interactor may call Schedule Interactor)
- View → view-model types + `constants/`
- `data/` → nothing in `app/`, `modules/`, or UI

Keep Interactor / Presenter math testable without rendering a screen.

## Emergence (Ch.12)

Runs all the tests; no duplication; expresses intent; fewest elements that still pass tests and keep SRP.

## Concurrency (Ch.13)

Prefer clear async boundaries. `useNow` owns the interval clock in Builders; don’t scatter `setInterval` in cards. Snapshot times (`now`) when status can go stale. Reanimated worklets are a different runtime — don’t pretend they share React state. OS thread material is N/A unless native modules truly use threads.

## Successive Refinement (Ch.14)

Make it work, then refine with the bar green. Feature branch; no broken main. Commit when the human asks.

## Smells and Heuristics (Ch.17)

Prefer MIT clone/dead-code tooling (`eslint-plugin-unicorn`; optional `jscpd` / `knip`). Avoid nested ternaries and lonely ifs when a clearer structure exists. Keep cyclomatic gates under Functions (Ch.3).
